course: religion-ethics
lesson: 7
title: The Ten Commandments
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: After leaving Egypt the Israelites came to Mount Sinai, and there God gave Moses ten laws written on two tablets of stone: the Ten Commandments.
explain: The first commandments are about loving God: no other gods, no idols, respect for God's name, and keeping the Sabbath holy.
explain: The rest are about loving your neighbour: honour your parents, do not murder, be faithful in marriage, do not steal, do not lie about others, and do not covet what is theirs.
explain: Jesus later said that hate in the heart is already the start of murder, so the commandments are not only about what we do but also about what we want and feel.
explain: For a child today they are a map for living together: rest one day a week, respect your parents, never take what is not yours, and do not spread lies about a classmate.
fact: God gave the Ten Commandments to Moses on Mount Sinai, written on two stone tablets.
fact: The first commandments are about loving God, and the rest about loving other people.
fact: The first commandment says to worship only the one true God; Christians believe nothing should take his place, not money, fame or things, and because loving God comes first, it shapes how they keep all the others.
fact: The commandment against idols forbids making carved images to worship; Orthodox and Catholics use icons and statues to help prayer, while some Protestants avoid them, so this point is disputed.
fact: «Do not misuse the name of the Lord» means speaking of God and holy things with respect, not as a swear word.
fact: The Sabbath is a day of rest and worship; most Christians keep Sunday, the day they believe Jesus rose from the dead, and Jews keep Saturday.
fact: «Honour your father and mother» includes respecting parents and caring for them when they are old.
fact: «Do not murder» means do not take an innocent human life; Jesus taught that hate in the heart is where murder begins.
fact: «Do not commit adultery» means husbands and wives keep their promise to be faithful to each other.
fact: «Do not steal» means do not take what belongs to someone else, even something small.
fact: «Do not give false witness» means do not tell lies about other people that harm their good name.
fact: «Do not covet» means do not long jealously for what belongs to your neighbour; jealousy can lead to the other wrongs.
exercise: Where did God give Moses the Ten Commandments? | On Mount Sinai | On the bank of the Nile | In Pharaoh's palace | In the Temple in Jerusalem
exercise: What were the Ten Commandments written on? | Two tablets of stone | A long roll of paper | The walls of a tent | A golden plate
exercise: How are the commandments usually divided? | The first ones about loving God, the rest about loving people | The first ones for kings and rulers, the rest for the slaves and servants | The first ones for men, the rest for women | The first ones for Egypt, the rest for Israel
exercise: What does the first commandment say? | Worship only the one true God | Never do any work at all on a Monday | Always obey the king, whatever he says | Pray five times every single day
exercise: Which modern things can people treat almost like a god? | Money, fame and possessions | Bread, water and air | Parents, teachers and friends | Rain, sun and snow
exercise: Why do Christians disagree about the commandment on idols? | Some use icons and statues to help prayer, others avoid them | Some think it is about food and others think it is about clothes | Some read it as about Egypt only | Some think it was added in the last century
exercise: What does «do not misuse the name of the Lord» ask of people? | To speak of God with respect and not use his name as a swear word | Never to say any word in church | To write God's name only in gold | To change your own name at baptism
exercise: Which day do most Christians keep as their day of rest? | Sunday | Saturday | Friday | Wednesday
exercise: Which day do Jews keep as the Sabbath? | Saturday | Sunday | Monday | Friday morning
exercise: What does honouring your father and mother include when they are old? | Caring for them | Leaving them to manage alone | Taking their money | Moving far away from them
exercise: What did Jesus say about the commandment «do not murder»? | Hate in the heart is where murder begins | It only applies to soldiers | It no longer matters today, now that we have police and laws | It is the least important commandment
exercise: What does «be faithful in marriage» mean? | Husbands and wives keep their promise to each other | Married people must never argue | Married people must wear matching rings | Married people must live in one town
exercise: Is taking a pencil from someone's desk without asking stealing? | Yes, because it belongs to someone else | No, because pencils are cheap and there are lots | No, as long as nobody at all sees you do it | Only if you take more than three of them at once
exercise: What does «do not give false witness» forbid? | Telling lies about others that harm their good name | Making any noise in court | Writing stories that are made up, even for fun in class | Keeping a diary of what others do
exercise: What does «do not covet» warn against? | Jealously wanting what belongs to someone else | Buying new things in a shop | Saving your pocket money | Admiring a beautiful sunset
exercise: Why is coveting called dangerous, though nobody sees it? | Jealousy can lead people to break the other commandments | It makes your eyes tired | It is only dangerous for kings | It costs a lot of money
test: my friend says the ten commandments were given to noah after the flood. is that right? | No, they were given to Moses on Mount Sinai | Yes, Noah got them with the rainbow | No, they were given to Abraham in a dream under the stars | Yes, but Moses copied them later
test: «it's only a rubber, she's got loads, chill» — your friend just pocketed it. Which commandment is it? | Do not steal | Do not covet only | Keep the Sabbath holy | Honour your father and mother
test: kid on the bus: «omg that's so stupid» then uses God's name as a swear. A Christian friend looks upset. Why? | Christians believe God's name should be spoken with respect | She is upset about the word «stupid» | She thinks swearing on a bus is illegal and they'll all be arrested | She wanted to sit in that seat
test: A Muslim classmate asks: «why do you go to church on Sunday, not Saturday like the Jews?» | Most Christians keep Sunday, the day Jesus rose from the dead | The Bible forbids church on Saturday | Sunday is the only day of the week that churches are allowed to open | Jews and Christians both keep Friday
test: «I never hit anyone so I've kept "do not murder", job done». What would Jesus add? | Hate in the heart is where murder begins, so anger matters too | Nothing, because hitting someone is the only wrong that rule is about | That the rule only applies to grown-ups | That the rule was cancelled long ago
test: Lily spreads a rumour that Tom cheated in the test, but he didn't. Which commandment does she break? | Do not give false witness against your neighbour | Do not steal | Do not murder | Do not make idols
test: «he's got the new console and I'm SO mad, he doesn't even deserve it». What is the danger here? | Coveting: jealousy that can lead to worse wrongs | Idolatry: loving and worshipping the console instead of God | Breaking the Sabbath by playing | Dishonouring your parents
test: my sister: «honour your parents means do whatever they say, forever, even when you're 50». Better answer? | It means respect and care for them, including when they are old | It means only obeying until you are ten | It means giving them all your money | It means never disagreeing with them about anything, even when they are wrong
test: A classmate with no religion asks: «aren't statues in churches idols?» | Catholics and Orthodox say icons help prayer and are not worshipped; some Protestants avoid them; it is disputed | Yes, every church statue is an idol | No, because statues are made of stone | Christians agree there are no statues anywhere
test: A judge asks: «Which commandment do the other nine depend on most, and why?» | The first, because loving God shapes how we treat everything else | The tenth, because it is the last one written and so sums up the rest | The fourth, because rest is most important | None, because they are all separate rules
test: «whats the point of a rest day, I could finish my project». What does the Sabbath teach? | Rest and worship are part of a good life, not wasted time | Work should never be done at all, on any day of the week, by anyone | Projects are forbidden by the Bible | Rest is only for very old people
exam: Identify the location at which, according to the Book of Exodus, the Decalogue was conferred upon Moses. | Mount Sinai | Mount Ararat | The banks of the River Nile | The Temple at Jerusalem
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the prohibition designated «Thou shalt not covet»? | It proscribes the jealous desire for that which belongs to another | It proscribes the purchase of goods on the Sabbath | It proscribes the speaking of falsehoods in a court | It proscribes the making of carved images
exam: It is given that a pupil disseminates an untrue allegation concerning a classmate. Determine the commandment thereby contravened. | Thou shalt not bear false witness against thy neighbour | Thou shalt not steal | Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy | Thou shalt have no other gods before me
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of the observance of the day of rest. | The majority of Christians observe Sunday, whereas Jews observe Saturday | All Christians and Jews alike observe Saturday exclusively, as Moses commanded | All Christians and Jews observe Sunday exclusively | Neither Christians nor Jews observe a day of rest
